@{ ViewData["Title"] = "How private messages are encrypted"; }
<div class="panel" style="max-width:760px;margin:24px auto 0;">
    <h1>End-to-end encrypted private messages</h1>
    <p class="muted">Plain language, exact claims — no marketing.</p>

    <h2 class="panel-title">The guarantee</h2>
    <p>
        Every private message is encrypted <strong>in your browser</strong> before it is sent, and can only be
        decrypted in the browser of you and the person you're messaging. Your private keys are generated on your
        device and are stored on our server only in a form that's <strong>wrapped (encrypted) with a key derived
        from your password</strong> — which we never see. We cannot decrypt your messages, and
        <strong>a stolen copy of our database is useless</strong> for reading them, to an attacker or to us.
    </p>

    <h2 class="panel-title">How it works</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>At sign-up your browser creates two keypairs (ECDH P-256 for encryption, ECDSA P-256 for signing).</li>
        <li>Only the <em>public</em> keys go to the server. The <em>private</em> keys are AES-256-GCM-wrapped under a
            key stretched from your password (PBKDF2, 600k iterations), plus a copy under your one-time recovery code.</li>
        <li>Each message is encrypted once with a random key; that key is wrapped to both the recipient and you, and
            the whole thing is signed so the recipient knows it's genuinely from you.</li>
        <li>Decryption happens on your device and is rendered as plain text — never as HTML.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="panel-title">What this does <em>not</em> protect (the honest limits)</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Trusting the code we serve.</strong> The encryption runs in JavaScript we send you each visit.
            "We can't read your messages" holds as long as that code is honest. We can't decrypt what's already stored,
            but a compromised or coerced server could ship altered code to a targeted user going forward. The client
            source is open for inspection.</li>
        <li><strong>Metadata.</strong> Encryption hides message <em>contents</em>, not who messaged whom or when.</li>
        <li><strong>Your password &amp; recovery code.</strong> The strength of the stored, wrapped keys is the strength
            of your password. If you forget your password <em>and</em> lose your recovery code, your past messages are
            gone for good — that's the proof no one else can read them either.</li>
        <li><strong>Forward secrecy.</strong> A database dump alone reveals nothing, but if someone also steals a
            recipient's unlocked device they could read messages that person already received.</li>
        <li><strong>No server-side search, previews, or moderation</strong> of message contents — the server can't read them.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="hint">Password changes automatically re-secure your keys. After a password <em>reset</em>, use your
        recovery code on the <a asp-controller="Account" asp-action="RecoverKeys">restore page</a> to regain access.</p>
</div>
